UPDATE challenges SET description = 'Permitting systems bundle multiple incommensurable purposes — safety review, environmental compliance, democratic notification, political discretion, rent protection, revenue generation — that are often genuinely in tension. But orthogonal to these purpose conflicts is a second problem: the cognitive and administrative burden imposed by coordination complexity itself. Each additional touchpoint, form, portal, and institutional handoff imposes load on applicants and administrators alike, independent of whether the underlying purposes conflict. 

The challenge: design a methodology to separate purpose-conflict friction from coordination-complexity friction in a real permit corpus. If the two are separable, it opens a tractable intervention space — agentic approaches that absorb coordination costs without requiring anyone to relinquish purposes they actually care about, and without the political economy of consolidation. The interesting result is not that permitting can be made simpler, but that its irreducible complexity can be made to fall on machines rather than people.'
 WHERE id = 10 AND description = 'Permitting systems bundle multiple incommensurable purposes — safety review, environmental compliance, democratic notification, political discretion, rent protection, revenue generation — that are often genuinely in tension. But orthogonal to these purpose conflicts is a second problem: the cognitive and administrative burden imposed by coordination complexity itself. Each additional touchpoint, form, portal, and institutional handoff imposes load on applicants and administrators alike, independent of whether the underlying purposes conflict. <br><br>The challenge: design a methodology to separate purpose-conflict friction from coordination-complexity friction in a real permit corpus. If the two are separable, it opens a tractable intervention space — agentic approaches that absorb coordination costs without requiring anyone to relinquish purposes they actually care about, and without the political economy of consolidation. The interesting result is not that permitting can be made simpler, but that its irreducible complexity can be made to fall on machines rather than people.';
